itor that I  had worked with and who was extremely  experienced and I knew we had to work  fast so that was again one of the  choices that we made and it was you know  I used I like to say I use  a lot of goodwill currency doing that  because unlike you know live-action we  see people day in and day out were there  on staff so it's not like people  disappear right but ultimately I felt  that that was the right thing to do for  the film and for Pete it was very much  planned from the beginning not just  because of the speed we had to go but  also because of the story that Pete was  seeing that it would be very little  dialogue and be an immersive film so the  story team and Kelsey and I and Pete we  all and Denise we all watched black  stallion together we all watched never  cry wolf together we watched a lot of  immersive films who watched a lot of oil  and dog films of course but at this  point we were watching a lot of  immersive films to see how how do you do  that how do you immerse the audience so  deeply in a place in a setting and then  with two characters or characters that  you're just happy and thrilled to watch  their behavior together so we did a lot  of that and then it really came down to  a lot to the storyboard artists because  they were the first ones in and after  the script you know I'm laying down the  stone so to speak you know I'm laying  down the spine and the stones and kind  of the emotional through line to make  sure the relationship will hold it needs  to hold on the page and all the logic  and so there's a lot of construction on  the page but then it has to go into  boards where really it has to come alive  because they're not talking so there's  you know they're doing the acting in  pictures and if it can work in boards we  knew it would only get amazing 100,000  times better once it was animated this  was also an animators dream to  ultimately work on this film and just it  was really remarkable to watch because  we had to work so fast you know Megan  the story team would kind of you know  put down the ideas and then the  animators would just plus it in a way  that was quite remarkable you know and  this is where Jeff and Michael really  come into play so much with this film  Pete and I knew that the music and the  sound design would be so incredibly  important to telling the emotional story  of our characters and it's really  fascinating because  a lot of comments have been that people  don't feel like it is there's a lot much  or is less dialogue as we we keep saying  and I think that's because the music and  the sound design does so such an  incredible job helping us with the story  I understand the idea of a lyricist and  a composer I don't know that I  understand how two composers work you  know I don't know obviously I suppose  the fact that your brothers helps that  you grew up and have kind of an  understanding of each other but how do  you compose together and do you divide  up the work or do you bounce things off  of each other what's the process well I  think we think of each other in a way as  an extension of ourself and we've been  working together so long obviously we  have careers separate from each other  but we go back as kids playing music  together so it's something that's just  absolutely second nature to us and and  we're very similar obviously we come  from the same place we listen to the  same music growing up our parents saying  and we heard also all of us have you  know there's so much unspoken already  that's already connecting us in that way  but we have different strengths and I  think when we first saw this film there  was such a opportunity for for a lot of  the strum stringed instruments that Jeff  plays he's got this incredible  collection of and he can tell you all  the names of them but bazoo keys and and  sizes and things from all over the world  that we wanted to honor the the kind of  folk  Western nature of it but not get too  cliche so we wanted to do that in an  interesting way right up Jeff's alley  and then I I'm more of a piano player  and so I played the kind of Grandma's  porch piano that you hear in there  sometimes and so I think for this movie  it just was something that that was  perfect for our collaboration and how  actually how we work is we you know we  just do it organically we'll say okay we  we knew that this film needed to be very  melodic and have strong melodies and  that's the thing we spent a lot of  time on that that's where two of us  working together really was a  strengthening thing that much like Arlo  and spot help each other their journey  that to lift their game you know one of  us would write for bars and then the  other one might finish it or he might  write the whole melody and the other one  go it's great but this note here you  know and I think it could be stronger  here and it just it's it's just kind of  a constant polishing and you get two  guys looking at it and standing back and  making it better it just gets better and  it really yeah lifts it to a level where  you know that it's it's beyond what  either of us could have done on our own  when we first looked at the film it's  it's a magnificent canvas for music I  mean I incredible to look at it's a  beautiful world they've created and then  there's not a lot of talking in some  scenes sequences there's just a lot of  space so we thought you know there's a  lot of different sort of levels of  storytelling going on here and you know  you move in and out of the Western thing  into the primitive world into some stuff  there's very deep emotional things and  the music has to track that whole  journey but still stay cohesive so we  knew there had to be an orchestral bed  that we built you know into right into  the foundation of this score but then we  wanted to heavily pepper it with these  other elements were maybe a bit  different so to honor the the natural  world we use a lot of pre-columbian  instruments some very primitive crazy  and stuff very kind of unmusical but we  would make it work somehow with the  orchestra like mike has said that there  was strum e instruments and stringed  instruments that we were able to use for  the western things and we didn't really  present that exactly normally so to  speak I mean like a lot of the things  that people think of banjos are actually  these Turkish guitars this thing called  a June Bush and it's all just a little  off a little left because they're not  really Cowboys they're dinosaurs so  homesteading dinosaurs frontier yes
 

